title: The duplicate scan
tags: Duplicates
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Four phases, each narrowing the candidates before the next, costlier one — size, partial hash, full hash, prune.

The scan (`daemon/src/duplicates.rs`) is an observable background task
([[Task kinds]]; stopping it keeps what it found, [[Cancelling a task]]). Four phases, each narrowing the
candidates before the next, more expensive one:

# ''size'' — every candidate file ([[What counts as a duplicate]]) is partitioned by `mfr_size`, and size classes of one are dropped. This is by far the biggest cut, so it is made on `(uuid, size)` alone, before anything heavier is built; only the survivors get a path resolved (and with it the offline-volume guard).
# ''partial'' — each survivor gets its `mfr_partial_hash`, reused when a stored one is still valid ([[The duplicate hash cache]]). Within a size class, a partial hash that occurs once eliminates its file.
# ''full'' — the files still in play get `mfr_full_hash`; files sharing (size, full hash) form a group. Size classes are processed ''largest first'', and each class's groups are written as it completes: a cancelled scan leaves a correct partial result holding the most valuable groups.
# ''prune'' — reached only when the others completed: removes the link from records no longer members, and deletes groups left with fewer than two. A cancelled scan skips it, so stale groups survive until the next complete run. A scan restricted to a subtree prunes only there.

!! What it reads

Five whole-repository questions to the store, before it hashes anything: the
candidates with their sizes and paths, the stored hashes and their stamps, the
`mfr_inode` owners, and the declared mount points. Then it ''lets the store
go'': hashing reads files, not the store, and holding the repository's lock
through it made every other request — the GUI's change feed first — wait for
the whole scan (15 s on 50 000 files, [[The session scenario]]).

!! What it writes

The scan keeps what it found in memory — ''the hash cache'' (`mfr_partial_hash`,
`mfr_full_hash`, `mfr_hash_mtime`, `mfr_hash_size`) and ''the groups'' of each size
class as it completes — and takes the store back to write it every twenty
seconds, and at the end with ''the prune''. All of it under one label.

Since the store was free in between, a write trusts nothing loaded before it:

* the existing groups with their counters and the member links are read again (two whole-repository questions per write, none per record) — a group deleted since is made again, a counter changed since is compared to its current value;
* a record deleted since gets no hash;
* each member of a group is checked on disk: a file whose `(mtime, size)` is no longer the one its full hash was taken under is left out of the group. Its change reaches the store after this write (the watcher's flush waits for the store too), and finds it in no group.

A hash written for a file changed since is harmless: its stamp no longer matches,
and the next scan computes it again ([[The duplicate hash cache]]).

The window is in ''time'': it bounds how many commits a scan makes — each is an
`fsync`; committing per 500 records and per size class made 503 writes of a scan of
50 000 files, 17 of its 31 s — and what an error or a crash can lose. A cancel loses
nothing, it writes what is pending first. Twenty seconds, not the watcher's two:
losing a window here costs only hashes to compute again, never a store out of step
with the files.

A field is written only when it is absent or changed, so a re-scan of an
unchanged repository writes nothing.

!! Progress

|!Phase |!Counts |
|`size` |files partitioned / candidate files |
|`partial` |files hashed / files to hash |
|`full` |''bytes'' hashed / bytes to hash |
|`prune` |groups checked / groups |

The `full` phase counts bytes because file sizes span orders of magnitude and a
file count would make the bar meaningless; its total is known exactly before it
starts, so a remaining-time estimate is an honest extrapolation. The `partial`
phase reads a bounded 8 KiB per file, near-constant, so files are the right unit
there.
